{"n":42,"paragraphs":["Swamiji liked to recall the old story of Solon, the wise man, and Croesus, the wealthy king.","Croesus once boasted to Solon that his land in Asia Minor was a very happy place. Solon asked him who the happiest man was, adding that he himself had never met anyone truly happy. Croesus brushed this aside. Obviously, he said, he was the happiest man in the world.","Solon told him not to be so quick. Wait until the end of your life before you decide, he said, and then he left.","Years later, the Persians conquered Croesus's kingdom and ordered him to be burnt alive. When the pyre was built and the king saw it, he cried out, \"Solon! Solon!\"","The Persian emperor wanted to know who he was calling. Croesus told him the whole story of his conversation with the sage. The emperor was so moved that he spared Croesus's life."],"learn":"Results, likes and grades can make us feel on top of the world, but circumstances change fast. Keep your sense of happiness rooted in something deeper than what you own or achieve, and stay humble on good days.","source":"Vol. 1, p. 409","images":["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]}